The biggest surprise this year was the sharp decline in Chipping Sparrows, which are usually a staple in my backyard. Then again, now that I think about it, I haven’t seen nearly as many this year. Maybe the breeding pair that typically nests in my backyard decided to call somewhere else home.
A couple of other things stood out to me. Some calls were classified as Fish Crow, which is certainly possible. However, it could also be the result of an update to BirdNET (the classifier I use to identify birds by their calls), as previous recordings were almost always identified as American Crow. Another interesting pattern was the relative increase in Blackpoll Warbler detections, which was a pleasant surprise.

I am an avid birder and some of my favorite places to bird include local, state, and federal public lands. Why public lands? Because they are (usually) open to the public, and provide unique opportunities to see some amazing wildlife (including birds)! Below you can select and view some of the public lands I’ve visited. You can even see the most recent bird sightings (last 2 weeks for up to 10 selected public lands), but you’ll need to enter your eBird API key and hit the Run button. If you don’t have an eBird API key, then visit this page.

